Patch 14: 35b9e7072c10 — "block: add submit_bio_noacct_fast and BIO_REGISTERED"
================================================================================

Two key additions to the block layer:

1. submit_bio_noacct_fast():
   A simplified bio submission path that bypasses most of the normal bio
   plumbing. For slot IOs, all the validation was already done at registration
   time, so we don't need to recheck things.

   The normal path submit_bio_noacct() does:
     - ->make_override (for things like dm/md)
     - cgroup tracking (blkcg)
     - bio_integrity_prep
     - bio_advance()/bio_trim() checks
     - various other checks

   The fast path submit_bio_noacct_fast() does:
     if (blk_queue_enter(q, flags) == 0)    // prevent queue removal
         blk_mq_submit_bio(bio)              // straight to the driver

   The function signature is:
     void submit_bio_noacct_fast(struct bio *bio, struct request_queue *q)

   Note that q is passed explicitly (not bio->bi_bdev->bd_queue) because the
   slot already knows the queue from registration time.

2. BIO_REGISTERED flag:
   A new bio->bi_opf flag: REQ_BIO_REGISTERED (0x200000000). This marks a bio
   as originating from a registered IO slot. It is set in io_slot_rw_issue()
   when configuring the slot bio:

     slot->bio.bi_opf = REQ_OP_READ | REQ_BIO_REGISTERED | REQ_IOCB_IOPOLL;

   The flag is used by:
     - The NVMe driver to select the fast DMA path (patch 5)
     - The block layer to propagate to RQF_REGISTERED on the request (patch 6)

3. Bio embedding:
   The slot embeds its bio directly (struct bio bio; in struct io_io_slot)
   rather than allocating one per IO. The bio's bvec array (bi_inline_vecs or
   the preallocated bi_io_vec pointing to imu->bvec) is set up once at
   registration time and reused for every IO on that slot.

   Per-IO submission only adjusts:
     - bi_iter.bi_sector (device offset)
     - bi_iter.bi_size (transfer length)
     - bi_opf (read/write + flags)

   The bi_io_vec entries stay the same — they always point to the registered
   buffer pages.

