Patch 13: fb4cde303c4d — "io_uring: add IORING_OP_SLOT_RW and wire up submit path"
================================================================================

Adds IORING_OP_SLOT_RW, the actual IO opcode for registered slot operations.

io_slot_rw_prep() — the prep handler (called during submission prep phase):
  - Reads the SQE fields: slot_id, rw_flags (read/write), buf_offset, bytes,
    and possibly a submit_offset (for concurrent IO)
  - Validates the slot exists (ctx->io_slots[slot_id] != NULL) and is active
    (slot->file != NULL)
  - Checks that the block device is opened for the right mode (read vs write)
  - If req->async_data is large enough, stores the slot pointer and offset there
    (avoids per-IO memory allocation — the io_kiocb already has async_data space)

io_slot_rw_issue() — the issue handler (called to actually submit the IO):
  - Sets bi_iter.bi_sector = offset into the device (computed from the file
    offset, not the buffer offset)
  - Sets bi_iter.bi_size = transfer length
  - Sets bi_opf = REQ_OP_READ/WRITE | BIO_REGISTERED | IOCB_IOPOLL
  - Calls submit_bio_noacct_fast() to submit
  - For iopoll, does NOT take uring_lock (unlike normal IO path)

The fact that it doesn't take uring_lock for iopoll is important: the slot
guarantees that only one IO is in flight at a time (via the slot->busy flag),
so there's no concurrency to protect against. The slot->busy flag acts as the
synchronization point instead.

The issue handler also handles the case where a slot IO needs an overflow bio
(see patch 10) — if the slot is already busy with an in-flight IO, a new
overflow bio is allocated and linked.

Slot IOs don't auto-plug (no request allocation needed since the slot has a
pre-allocated req). The slot->req is a struct io_kiocb embedded in the slot,
allocated from a dedicated kmem_cache (slot_cache). It has REQ_F_IOPOLL set
at allocation time. This is the "stack-allocated kiocb" concept — it lives
for the lifetime of the slot, not per-IO.

