Patch 12: 45fb9c612e31 — "io_uring: add support for registered io slots"
================================================================================

The core slot registration infrastructure. This is where the main data
structures and the registration ioctl are defined.

io_register_io_slot() — the IORING_REGISTER_IO_SLOT handler:

  1. Copies struct io_uring_io_slot_reg from userspace:
     struct io_uring_io_slot_reg {
         __u16 slot_id;
         __u16 rw_flags;            // read or write
         __s32 buf_ring_fd;         // fd of the registered buffer ring
         __u32 buf_ring_offset;     // offset into the buffer ring
         __s32 file_fd;             // fd of the block device
         __u64 __rsrvd[2];
     };

  2. Validates:
     - slot_id is in range [0, ctx->nr_io_slots)
     - ctx->io_slots[slot_id] is not already registered
     - buf_ring_fd refers to a valid registered buffer ring
     - file_fd refers to a valid block device file opened O_DIRECT
     - The buffer ring has enough space for the slot

  3. Gets or creates the io_mapped_ubuf (imu) for the buffer ring. The imu
     describes the pre-registered buffer's page layout (nr_bvecs bvec entries,
     total length, folio_shift, etc.)

  4. Gets the file and its bdev (block device) from file_fd

  5. Allocates struct io_io_slot, fills it in, stores in ctx->io_slots[slot_id]

  6. Calls io_slot_alloc_bio() to set up slot->bio

  7. Calls io_buf_get_dma() to create the persistent DMA mapping

struct io_io_slot — the per-slot data structure (internal to slot.c):

  struct io_io_slot {
      struct file          *file;         // bdev file from registration
      struct io_mapped_ubuf *imu;         // registered buffer (the backing pages)
      struct bio           bio;           // embedded bio, reused per IO
      struct io_slot_dma   *dma;          // persistent DMA mapping
      struct io_kiocb      req;           // pre-allocated kiocb
      int                  slot_id;       // index into ctx->io_slots[]
      unsigned int         nr_bvec;       // number of bvec entries
      atomic_t             busy;          // 1 = IO in flight, 0 = idle
  };

The busy flag semantics:
  - atomically set to 1 at submission time (test_and_set)
  - atomically cleared to 0 at completion time
  - Prevents concurrent submissions to the same slot
  - Also used as the signal for "need to allocate overflow bio" — if busy was
    already 1 when trying to submit, allocate an overflow bio instead of failing

io_slot_alloc_bio() — initializes the slot's embedded bio:

  slot->bio.bi_io_vec = imu->bvec;        // reuse the registered buffer's bvec
  slot->bio.bi_vcnt = imu->nr_bvecs;
  slot->bio.bi_max_vecs = imu->nr_bvecs;
  bio_set_dev(&slot->bio, bdev);          // associate with the block device
  slot->bio.bi_private = dma;             // for bio_persistent_dma()

The slab cache for slots:

  static struct kmem_cache *slot_cache;
  // allocated at module init via:
  // slot_cache = KMEM_CACHE(io_io_slot, SLAB_HWCACHE_ALIGN | SLAB_PANIC);

Slot unregistration (io_free_io_slot):
  - io_buf_put_dma() to release the persistent DMA mapping
  - fput(file) to release the bdev file
  - io_put_rsrc_node() for the buffer ring
  - kmem_cache_free(slot_cache, slot)
  - ctx->io_slots[slot_id] = NULL

The slot table:
  - ctx->io_slots is a flexible array at the end of struct io_ring_ctx:
      struct io_io_slot *io_slots[] __counted_by(nr_io_slots);
  - nr_io_slots is set at ring creation time based on the IORING_SETUP_IO_SLOTS
    flag and the slots field in struct io_uring_params
  - Maximum number of slots is limited (typically 4096 or so)

Questions that came up:

Q: The slot->req is allocated from slot_cache, but when does it get freed?
A: When the slot is unregistered. The slot->req isn't a separate allocation —
   it's embedded in struct io_io_slot (the struct has a struct io_kiocb req
   field), so it lives and dies with the slot. No per-IO allocation needed.

Q: What guarantees slot_id validity across a re-register?
A: When a slot is unregistered, its slot_id is freed. A new registration can
   reuse the same slot_id. The register ioctl checks ctx->io_slots[slot_id]
   == NULL before allocating, so you can't double-register. If you register,
   unregister, and re-register the same slot_id, the old slot is fully torn
   down before the new one is created.

