Patch 10: 98ecbf760f54 — "io_uring: add support for concurrent IO on registered slots"
================================================================================

Normally each registered slot allows only one in-flight IO (serialized by the
slot->busy flag). This patch adds overflow bio support so that concurrent IOs
targeting the same slot can proceed without blocking.

The overflow mechanism:

  1. When an IO arrives and slot->busy is already 1 (an IO is in flight), the
     submission code calls io_slot_alloc_overflow() instead of failing.

  2. io_slot_alloc_overflow() allocates a new bio from the filesystem's bio_set:
     struct bio *io_slot_alloc_overflow(struct io_io_slot *slot)
     {
         struct bio *bio = bio_alloc_bioset(bdev, slot->nr_bvec, ...);
         // copy bvec layout from slot->bio
         bio->bi_io_vec = slot->bio.bi_io_vec;  // reuse same bvec array
         bio->bi_vcnt = slot->bio.bi_vcnt;
         // store the overflow bio in a linked list on the slot
         list_add_tail(&bio->bi_list, &slot->overflow_bios);
         return bio;
     }

  3. Overflow bios share the same DMA mapping (via refcount on io_buf_dma).
     They don't create new DMA mappings — they just use the existing one.

  4. On completion, both the main bio and any overflow bios are completed.
     The completion walks the overflow list:
     list_for_each_entry_safe(bio, tmp, &slot->overflow_bios, bi_list) {
         bio->bi_status = status;
         bio_endio(bio);
     }

  5. The overflow bios are freed after completion (bio_put or bio_uninit).

The slot still enforces one IO at a time via the busy flag — the difference is
that instead of failing, it now queues the new IO as an overflow that will be
submitted once the current IO completes. The actual parallelism comes from
having multiple IOs in flight on different slots, but within a single slot,
multiple concurrent submissions are pipelined via the overflow list.

Wait — actually, re-reading the patch more carefully: the overflow bios are
submitted concurrently with the main bio, not queued for later. The busy flag
guards the slot struct, not the device. The different overflow bios can be in
flight simultaneously because they target different sectors of the same buffer
and can complete independently. The DMA mapping is shared (refcounted), so
it stays alive as long as any overflow bio references it.

