Patch 7: f9cbe1a9b134 — "block: add bio_persistent_dma() helper"
================================================================================

Adds a helper to the block layer so drivers can retrieve the persistent DMA
mapping from a registered bio:

  struct io_slot_dma *bio_persistent_dma(struct bio *bio)
  {
      return bio->bi_private;
  }

This is used by the NVMe driver's fast path (patch 5) to get the pre-computed
DMA mapping from a registered IO's bio.

The bi_private field was already present on struct bio (used for various things
like fs-layer private data). The slot code sets it at registration time:

  slot->bio.bi_private = slot->dma;   // the persistent DMA mapping

This patch creates the reverse dependency problem:
  - include/linux/bio.h needs to declare: struct io_slot_dma *bio_persistent_dma(...)
  - But struct io_slot_dma is defined in include/linux/io_uring_types.h
  - bio.h can't include io_uring_types.h (that would make every block layer
    user depend on io_uring)
  - Solution in the original series: forward-declare struct io_slot_dma and
    include io_uring_types.h only in the .c files that need it

In the original code, bio.h uses a forward declaration:

  struct io_slot_dma;
  struct io_slot_dma *bio_persistent_dma(struct bio *bio);

This works in C: you can return a pointer to an incomplete type as long as
you don't dereference it. The caller (NVMe driver pci.c) includes
io_uring_types.h to get the full struct definition so it can access members
like dma->use_iova, dma->state.addr, etc.

The forward declaration approach in C:
  - A function declaration can use a pointer-to-incomplete-type as a parameter
    or return type without the full definition being visible
  - The caller must see the full definition before dereferencing the pointer
  - This is different from C++ where the type must be complete in more contexts

