Patch 4: 34b074eaaa3a — "nvme-pci: use SGL fast path for any-size registered IO"
================================================================================

Extends the registered fast path from patch 5 to handle IOs larger than 2 NVMe
pages by using a single SGL data descriptor.

Before (patch 5):

  if (total > NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE * 2 - prp1_offset)
      return BLK_STS_AGAIN;   // too big for 2 PRPs, fall back

After (patch 4):

  if (total > NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE * 2 - prp1_offset) {
      struct nvme_queue *nvmeq = req->mq_hctx->driver_data;

      // No SGL support (admin queue or PRP-only controller)?
      if (!nvmeq->qid || !nvme_ctrl_sgl_supported(&nvmeq->dev->ctrl))
          return BLK_STS_AGAIN;

      // SGL fast path: single descriptor, no DMA walk
      iod->total_len = total;
      iod->flags |= IOD_SINGLE_SEGMENT;
      iod->cmd.common.flags = NVME_CMD_SGL_METABUF;
      iod->cmd.common.dptr.sgl.addr  = cpu_to_le64(dma_addr);
      iod->cmd.common.dptr.sgl.length = cpu_to_le32(total);
      iod->cmd.common.dptr.sgl.type  = NVME_SGL_FMT_DATA_DESC << 4;
      return BLK_STS_OK;
  }

What is SGL? NVMe Scatter-Gather List format. The NVMe command can use either
PRP (physical region page) or SGL (scatter-gather list) to describe data
buffers. SGL is more flexible: a single SGL data descriptor can describe up
to 2^32 bytes with a single (address, length) pair.

The check nvmeq->qid ensures SGL is only used for IO queues:
  - qid=0 is the admin queue which never uses SGL
  - qid>0 is an IO queue

The controller must advertise SGL support via the SGL Support field in the
Controller Capabilities (CAP) register. nvme_ctrl_sgl_supported() checks this.

SGL vs PRP performance:
  - PRP doesn't require an extra DMA read — the PRP1 and PRP2 fields are
    directly in the 64-byte submission queue entry (SQE). For small transfers
    (≤2 pages), PRP is faster because there's no descriptor fetch.
  - SGL requires the controller to DMA-read the SGL descriptor(s) from host
    memory. For large transfers, this is negligible compared to the data xfer.
  - The real win in this patch isn't SGL vs PRP — it's avoiding dma_map_page()
    entirely. Whether you emit PRP or SGL, you use the same pre-computed
    single DMA address, saving the per-IO DMA mapping of each page.

Remaining limitation after this patch:
  - PRP-only controllers with IOs >2 pages still fall back (BLK_STS_AGAIN)
  - Multi-segment direct path (nr_segs > 1) still falls back
  - Both are fixed by patch 1 (precomputed PRP list) and further work

